          "files_to_modify": ["awesome_researcher/parsers/markdown_parser.py"],
          "files_to_create": [],
          "patterns_from": ["awesome_researcher/parsers/markdown_parser.py", "awesome_researcher/parsers/awesome_parser.py"],
          "verification": {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "cd /Users/nick/awesome-researcher && python -c \"from awesome_researcher.parsers.markdown_parser import parse_markdown; p = parse_markdown('## Web\\n\\n<a href=\\\"https://example.com\\\">Example</a> - A site.\\n'); c = p.categories[0]; assert c.items and c.items[0].url == 'https://example.com'; assert c.items[0].raw_line.startswith('<a'); print('OK')\"",
            "expected": "OK"
          },
          "status": "pending"
        },
        {
          "id": "subtask-1-2",
          "title": "Fixture-driven byte-exact round-trip tests",
          "description": "Create tests/test_phase3_roundtrip_fixtures.py: for each of tests/fixtures/awesome-go.README.md, awesome-python.README.md, awesome-machine-learning.README.md, parse with parse_markdown, run SourcePatcher().patch(parsed) with zero inserts, and assert output == source bytes exactly. Also assert each fixture yields a non-trivial parse (>50 categories+items) and that inserting one item into a real category leaves every non-inserted byte unchanged (diff via difflib shows only added lines). Follow the offline, real-fixture test pattern of tests/test_phase3_parsers.py.",
